bug_report:
[11/12/13 Regression] g++ allows direct-initialization of an array of class type from another array in a mem-initializer
Reduced testcase:

  struct string {} a[1];
  struct pair {
    string s[1]; pair() : s(a) {}
  };

This is ill-formed, but g++ accepts it. The initialization is rejected if we try to direct-initialize a non-member array this way, or if 'string' is replaced by a non-class type, so I assume this is a bug rather than a deliberate extension.
